{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786969131,"nonce":"2YVbv9fTvwtDNKYGXwaeMukNWqc=","title":"Page headings are a type step and nothing else, so the brush the brand is built on appears on no screen an operator works in","message":"**The demonstration.** Every page heading in the product is `text-page` (18 px) and a font weight — `web/src/routes/alerts.tsx`, `groups.tsx`, `settings.tsx`, `notifications.tsx` all render the same unadorned string. §M.8 gives `--oto-type-page` its own step and notes it is \"the page heading, and nothing else\", so the ladder already treats it as singular; nothing in the chrome does. The brush that the brand sheet is built on — the same hand that drew the wordmark in the rail — appears nowhere an operator actually works.\n\n**Why it matters.** ADR 0031 took the interface to sharp corners and a crisp technical grid, which is the right call for a table an operator reads at 3am and which leaves the product with no surviving trace of the ink-painting language ADR 0030 chose. The design study converged on headings as the one place a brush belongs: a page heading is singular, it appears once per screen, and it is the only text in the product with enough contrast headroom to wear ink.\n\nThat headroom is the whole argument and it is measurable. `--oto-text` sits at 14.24:1 on `--oto-bg` in light and 14.07:1 in dark, so a 12% tint behind it lands at 11.36:1 and even 24% holds at 8.81:1 — against a 4.5:1 floor, because 18 px at weight 600 is *not* \"large text\" (that starts at 18.66 px bold). The same treatment behind `--oto-text-muted` body copy would be a defect and behind `--oto-text-subtle` an outright failure: subtle starts at 4.90:1 and fails AA under a 6% wash. **The rule is that ink goes behind the heading and nowhere else**, and it is worth writing down in that form because the tempting next step is a wash behind a section label.\n\nTwo shapes and two hues, all four legal, all Tier A. The background is a **two-pass swipe** — two overlapping brush passes with the seam left visible; the underline is a **smooth tapered rule**. They are separate assets and must stay separate: a mask has no fixed aspect, and stretching the thin rule to heading height is exactly what produces a shape that reads as a wave rather than a stroke. The hue is `--oto-border-strong` or `--oto-accent`; both are chrome under §M.2, so neither can be mistaken for a state. Worth knowing before choosing: the two hues are clearly distinct in light (kincha gold against beni crimson) and nearly collapse in dark, where ADR 0032 put the accent at ~233° next to a blue-grey border token.\n\nThe brush also overshoots the word, asymmetrically — roughly a 7 px lead and a 22 px tail. A brush enters just before the first letter and lifts off well after the last; matching the two makes it read as a box rule again.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/routes/alerts.tsx`, `groups.tsx`, `settings.tsx`, `notifications.tsx`, `alert-detail.tsx` (the page headings); `web/src/components/ui/surfaces.tsx:28` (`SECTION_LABEL`) and `:48-50` (`PanelTitle`) — **explicitly out of scope**, see below; `docs/design/SPEC.md:5237+` (§M.8, `--oto-type-page`); `docs/adr/0031-sharp-corners-and-crisper-grid.md` (the geometry this coexists with rather than contradicts); `docs/adr/0032-plum-blossom-blue-dark-accent.md` (why the accent hue reads differently per theme); the primitive ticket's `--oto-wash` and `@utility`.\n\n**Scope.** small — two SVG assets and one heading component, plus a token for the heading-weight tint, which is heavier than `--oto-wash` and needs its own step.\n\n**Done when.** Page headings can carry a brush, background or underline, in muted or accent, from two purpose-drawn assets rather than one stretched one. The tint is a token, not a literal, and the heading's measured contrast is recorded somewhere a reader will find it. **Panel headers stay ruled** — `PanelHeader` and `SECTION_LABEL` do not get this, because alert detail stacks six panels and at six a gesture becomes a texture, which is the failure mode the whole study was trying to avoid. The rule that ink goes behind a page heading and nowhere else is written down, not merely observed.","files":null}]}